### Runnable examples for every endpoint.
### VS Code: install the "REST Client" extension, then click Send Request.
### JetBrains IDEs: built in.
###
### Start the server first:  npm run seed && npm start

@host = http://localhost:3000

### Availability — after seeding this shows 6 capacity, 3 confirmed, 2 held.
### One unit free. A fourth unit is held by an already-expired hold, which
### does not count, because expiry is derived from the clock.
GET {{host}}/availability?store=S412&format=aisle_barrier&cycle=C5

### A store with plenty of space
GET {{host}}/availability?store=S733&format=trolley_panel&cycle=C5

### Unknown store — 404 rather than a guess
GET {{host}}/availability?store=S999&format=aisle_barrier&cycle=C5

### Trader A takes the last unit at S412
POST {{host}}/holds
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "campaignId": "kelloggs-spring",
  "storeId": "S412",
  "formatId": "aisle_barrier",
  "cycleId": "C5",
  "quantity": 1,
  "traderId": "trader-a"
}

### Trader B wants the same unit — 409, with the current availability so the
### trader's screen can update without a second call.
POST {{host}}/holds
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "campaignId": "nestle-cereal",
  "storeId": "S412",
  "formatId": "aisle_barrier",
  "cycleId": "C5",
  "quantity": 1,
  "traderId": "trader-b"
}

### A larger hold somewhere with room
POST {{host}}/holds
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "campaignId": "pepsi-summer",
  "storeId": "S733",
  "formatId": "trolley_panel",
  "cycleId": "C5",
  "quantity": 4,
  "traderId": "trader-c"
}

### Release a hold. Soft — the row is kept with who released it and why,
### because someone will ask in three weeks why a campaign lost a store.
### Change the id to one returned above.
DELETE {{host}}/holds/6
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "releasedBy": "manager-1",
  "reason": "Nestle deal takes priority for this cycle"
}

### Confirm a campaign. Returns both a confirmed count and a rejected list —
### partial success is a normal outcome, not an error.
POST {{host}}/campaigns/kelloggs-spring/confirm
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "confirmedBy": "trader-a"
}

### Confirm with deliberate overselling. The brief says commercial teams
### sometimes want to accept the risk, so this is allowed, flagged on the
### booking row, and reportable — rather than made impossible and worked
### around in spreadsheets.
POST {{host}}/campaigns/nestle-cereal/confirm?allowOversell=true
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "confirmedBy": "manager-1"
}
